      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orlean%2C_Virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The village’s oldest building, the Orlean Farm House, was constructed between 1795 and 1812 by the Smith family.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.dhr.virginia.gov/historic-registers/030-5159/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "John Puller Smith finished the house and helped launch Orlean’s first commercial center, making it the county’s first post office in 1817.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.dhr.virginia.gov/historic-registers/030-5159/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Orlean lies roughly 50 miles west of Washington, D.C., offering a rural escape just outside the capital.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orlean%2C_Virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Surrounded by pristine rolling farmland, Orlean was once part of Thomas Lord Fairfax’s expansive Leeds Manor.",
